  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 121 PM MST Sun Nov 22 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nAs the upper level trough exits to the east, we have one more \ncold day today before a gradual warming trend through the rest of \nthe week. Light snow showers will be possible going into this \nevening, before chances become mostly confined to Southwest \nMontana early tomorrow morning. Fog is looking to develop tonight \nacross the northern plains and lower elevations of Central and \nSouthwest Montana, and may be dense at times. While a dense fog \nadvisory has not been issued at this time, updated data this \nafternoon will warrant another look, and one may be issued later \ntoday.\n\nA shortwave trough quickly moves through Monday night as a closed\nlow digs southeastward from north of the Canadian border. While \nthe bulk of the vorticity (energy) will miss us to the east, \nstrong winds aloft in northwest flow will bring widespread breezy \nconditions on Tuesday. By that afternoon, upper level ridging will\nhave begun to move in, allowing for skies to clear. The ridging \nis short lived, however, as by Wednesday afternoon it begins to \nbreak down and flatten. A tightening surface pressure gradient \nover Central and North-Central Montana will create another day of \nbreezy conditions for that area. \n\nThe strongest winds of the week will be on Thursday and Friday, \nas the jet stream begins to move southward over the Continental \nUS. This will bring strong winds aloft, creating gusty winds at \nthe surface. By Saturday, the jet stream will have moved far \nenough southeast to form a large upper level trough spanning the \neastern half of the Continental US, which will put us back into \nNorthwest flow aloft. -Dzomba\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nLight winds and an existing snowpack promote patchy fog \ndeveloping this evening through tomorrow morning. Dewpoint \ndepressions throughout the northern plains dip down into the \nsingle digits, creating high relative humidity approaching, if not\nalready reaching, saturation. Relative humidity in many of the \nvalleys and lower elevations of Central Montana will also be \napproaching saturation. Fog development is also supported by \nsounding analysis, with many model soundings showing low level \ninversions occurring tonight. The fog that develops could be dense\nat times. The uncertainty in this fog development lies in two \nmain factors: the winds and the cloud cover. The winds tonight, \nwhile light, may be just strong enough to prevent fog from \nforming, and lingering low stratus may persist through skies \ntrying to clear. As of writing this AFD, a 20-40% chance of fog \nexists for portions of the northern plains and the lower \nelevations of Central and Southwest Montana. -Dzomba",
